h encodes the  program that you see here on the left  along with the assertion um  and this the goal of the synthesizer is  to  come up with an expression like the one  you see at the bottom left um  using a syntactic specification which is  shown at the top  right so that's the grammar of  invariants that we'd like to  synthesize and the verifier can be an  smt solver such as z3  okay  another example  is constraint solving so in this case  you have a different formulation of the  same  problem i showed on the previous slide  except that now instead of a smt solver  we might want to use a chc solver  and the input looks very different this  time  it is still a verification condition but  now these are all  uh horn constraints and the output  grammar  is still the same um and here's an  example  output which a chc solver like spacer  would  accept as a candidate  for the predicate itp that you see here  so idp is a binary relation over  integers  and the goal is to synthesize a program  that conforms to this grammar  and furthermore that a chc solver  can conclude  is makes this formula valid  okay so um  yet another application and the one  which i'll focus primarily throughout  this talk  it comes from the ai domain  it's called referring expression  generation it was originally proposed in  2014 uh by the paper i've listed at the  bottom  uh titled refer it game  and the idea here is that  it is too challenging for today's ai  architectures to describe let's say a  scene  and so there's a simple challenge which  is  if we refer to a certain object in the  scene at least then can we  can we name that object  so it's a more local task and then the  task was further simplified by  folks at stanford who designed the  clever data set  that some of you might have heard of  where to further reduce the noise  they went from real world images to  synthetic images like the one you see  here  nevertheless this is still a very  challenging task even though  you see only synthetic objects in this  scene and the goal  is to uniquely identify  a particular object in this case  the object label g  um the reason this task can be very  challenging is  uh you might need logical reasoning  in order to come up with an expression  that uniquely  names this target object  so here the specification is you are  given  an image along with a desired output  which is  the object that is labeled in this case  g  you're given a grammar for the program  we'd like to synthesize  in this case it is a conjunction of  clauses of the form you see here um  you can for example uh say  uh talk about the colors of objects or  their shapes or their materials and  their relative positions  and so on  and in this case the verifier can be um  something like a data log solver um  where you can execute um these  relational programs and check  whether they produce the desired object  as the value of a distinguished variable  which i will call  var0 okay so in summary here the goal is  come up with a  a single rule data log program  um where which when evaluated on  a relational representation of objects  in this image  uh will uh produce a single  uh object as the value of  var0 and that object should be the one  that is  labeled in the specification in this  case  g  so um what is the problem with applying  conventional search techniques right  um and i've listed a few of them here um  the main ones uh the syntax guided  enumeration where you  can enumerate these candidate programs  according to  uh the syntax of  the programs to be synthesized a classic  example is  uh e-solver  there are other approaches for example  there's an approach that we have dubbed  example guided enumeration  and you already see some some uh  instantiations in the literature such as  flash fill and flash relate  which um which look for patterns in the  in the input  data in order to guess the candidate  programs  we have a paper coming up at pldi called  example guided  synthesis egs which  uh shows how to for example generate uh  relational programs uh like sql queries  uh using this idea as well  and then there's evolutionary or  stochastic and genetic algorithms um  examples being stoke which  is a super optimizer  for optimizing small  pieces of code into assembly  um there are many optimizations in the  literature to speed up  this search i've listed some of them  here such as  indistinguishability which is used in  e-solver just work on probabilistic  models to  to prioritize the search based on  likelihood  of the programs  and and so on  so we set out to understand what are  some  desirable features of a search technique  that we'd like to have and we came up  with these five  features the first is it should be easy  to design  you know extend the search algorithm  right for example we should be able to  go from a linear invariant generator to  a non-linear invariant one by simply  changing the grammar  of the programs of the invariant right  uh another desirable feature of course  is scalability  um yet another which is somewhat  overlooked in the literature  is that the programs should be natural  or they should  have some measure of quality okay  and i can give an example here if you  look at the  the software verification competition  it is acceptable or even the saiges  competition for example  it's okay to produce programs that are  very unnatural as long as they satisfy  the verifier  um furthermore the last two are very  uh relevant to machine learning uh which  is  uh we want the search to become a  smarter  uh over time uh across different  instances  and also a related aspect is we would  like it to  be able to adapt  its search strategy to new instances  okay so learning across different  instances means avoiding mistakes  from the past and and it might be that  new instances could be  very different from those you have seen  in the past and so we'd like the search  to  adapt itself  so uh we can look at uh what has been  done  in uh machine learning in particular in  reinforcement learning  so there have been a lot of successes um  in the context of  uh strategy game playing so you've heard  of for example  alphago and and so on um  for chess go and and many other games  right um in this case uh however the  the rules for strategy games are fairly  simple  and the environment  gives scores based on the  outcome of applying these rules  whereas in program synthesis we are  given  a a syntactic specification which is the  grammar  of the program to be synthesized and a  semantic  uh specification which is uh a verifier  such as z3 or spacer or a data log  solver as i just showed  so these differences um pose new  challenges  uh both in terms of how to uh  represent a given program synthesis  instance and in terms of how to uh  conduct the search okay  so in particular  if you think about the state  that you'd like to represent in the case  of games  it is typically a 2d  matrix of lopix cells uh and the  the neural architectures are well known  for example convolutional networks and  so on  but for programs there's much richer  structure  um and um one might wonder what is the  best  uh neural architecture and  uh we have come up with uh one which is  uh uh called graph neural networks  um that was um  discovered by um you know  others in the past so we have just  applied graph neural networks to this  problem  um but there are many other  architectures as i'll talk about later  for example transformer models  viewing a program as a set of paths and  so on that are also  relevant here there are also challenges  in terms of reinforcement learning so  for games the space of actions is fixed  and finite  and there is often  a smooth reward that you can come up  with  in order to learn um the policy  efficiently  whereas for programs you have a context  free grammar to guide  the search and the reward can be  extremely sparse  so typically a solver will only say yes  or no  when a candidate program is proposed  okay  it typically has more information but  you converting that into a smooth  reward is can be challenging  so here's an approach that we have come  up with  and in fact we have um  [Music]  infrastructure that is open source that  you can use to  to cast your  favorite problem in this setting as well  um  so the idea here is um to provide a way  to  to represent your  specifications symbolically and that  becomes the state  um then we have a neural network which  is the synthesizer  that will perform a series of actions  um and thereby producing a series of  partial programs  that can be executed by  the environment in this case verifier  and what we do next is we both  update the state and we uh give a reward  to the neural  network um and the process repeats  okay so if you squint at this figure you  will see the sieges  loop over here  so what i'm going to do is take a short  detour from my slides and show you  a demo before proceeding further  so you can go to this website go to in  dot org  um and try out some of these examples  let me pick one of them here so you see  this is a program like the one i showed  earlier  it is converted into this task  so here's the specification verification  condition  and here's the grammar of  the looping variants we'd like to  generate  and what we do is we convert this  verification condition into a graph  because we are going to need this  symbolic representation  um for our  reinforcement learning framework  so this is again for the user to design  okay so um we have come up with a  particular way to  encode vc's into graphs and i'll  talk about more about that shortly  and with these inputs you can  run the framework and it's starting from  scratch here with all the weights  untrained  and what you'll see after a while is a  candidate  in variant you might say that this is  unnatural in many ways but  this is purely because of certain  limitations  that are very easy to do post-processing  or regularizing and so on  and what you have seen here is the  number of iterations of the cds loop so  there were 17 calls to  the verifier in this case  and you can also see a larger number of  tests for the pre  and post condition checks and the  inductive invariant check  and what you see here is um the result  of partial programs being generated so  in this even though there were only 17  iterations of the sieges  loop there were many more uh  checks of partial programs that were  needed to  get a smooth reward okay  you can also use the same framework for  in this case  i'm showing you chc formally  and and here's the graph encoding this  time it's different than what i showed  earlier  the grammar is still the same but you  could use a different grammar if you  wanted  and in this case now the once again the  framework is running but  using uh a chc solver  instead of an smp solver  okay hopefully it will  converge soon enough before i return to  my slides  please feel free to ask any questions  while  we let it finish  okay so here we have the final  expression in this case you know z3  happens to have a chc  solver as well so there were nine calls  made  so anyway the the framework is general  enough  what i showed you in fact was exactly  the same underlying neural architecture  in both of these cases  [Music]  and that goes back to  one of the the nice things about this  approach which is it's very easy to  to to adapt it to different tasks  without having to provide any hand  engineered heuristics and so on  so in the rest of my talk i'm just going  to go over  you know one particular example which is  the referred example  let us start with the symbolic  representation right so how do we  provide this representation i am going  to  skip some of this in the interest of  time  the point here is we want to go from  this rich structured  representation of programs to  um an embedding right a vector  of reals um and as i said earlier we use  a graph neural network for this purpose  okay um i won't have time to go into  what graph neural networks are  um but the idea intuitively is um  that doesn't the the  embedding here is uh essentially  some combination of the embeddings of  the nodes in the graph  and those node embeddings are computed  by  message passing so there are features  about the node itself but then  the more you iterate over this embedding  each node now has information about  the nodes farther and farther away from  it in the graph  so it has more and more context  information  since we did this work there has been  the emergence of transformer models so  what i said here  is not necessarily true today and in  fact we ourselves are  working with not just graph neural  networks but also trying out  these sequential models the transformer  models  and there's work between these two  extremes as well so instead of treating  programs as a sequence of tokens or as a  graph  you can have representations which treat  programs as a set of paths for example a  lot of work by  iran yahoo falls in that category  and there are interesting tradeoffs in  these different  representations but all of this work is  all  about graph neural networks  in the case of um the referred problem  uh the  the representation is relatively simpler  once again you see  the objects in the scene being  represented by  various nodes and their types of these  nodes  along with nodes for the attributes and  relationships between them and so on  okay so i'm not going to go too much  into the detail but  this is one of the limitations of this  approach which is you still have to  design  the symbolic representation  but once you have that much of the  the architecture is automated  right and i'm just going to show a run  of this loop  next so the goal of the neural network  at each step is to  select uh an action  in this grammar right so pick a  production rule um  in in the case of the refereed problem  it is to pick clauses  right and uh they are picked according  to the representation of the  specification  and the grammar that is given so both uh  this semantic and the syntactic parts of  the  task and let us say  the first clause picked is that the  target object should be  a gray colored object okay so this is a  candidate program that is generated  we then execute this program using the  verifier  there are many ways to design the reward  and this is the only other part that is  needs to be manually designed besides  the symbolic representation  in this case it turned out to be that a  very sparse reward  is good enough we did try richer  smoother rewards but they didn't give  much benefit  over the simple approach where if the  target variable variable 0 denotes  exactly  the object of interest in this case g  then 1  otherwise 0 so in this particular case  we get  0 reward because var 0  denotes every gray object in the scene  and you can see  that there's c h and g right  so what we do next is we update  the state uh based on uh the output of  the current program  so var0 now points to c h or g  okay so one thing you notice here  is that by representing the state in  this manner  we are capturing uh  an infinite set of different programs  that all  evaluate var0 to these  three objects okay and the same holds  for bar one and bar two and so on  which i'm not showing here  and then we simply repeat so the next  clause um  um is uh that  var zero is in front of bar one and then  uh the next clause is that var1 is a  brown object  right and at that point we have um  gotten a reward of one because var0  points to  the the unique desired object g  okay so  we evaluated this approach on the clever  data set which i mentioned  earlier and we also created a diagnostic  data set which is even more  complex in terms of reasoning i've shown  two such  examples here the first class is three  one one  one one which means that we have seven  objects out of which  three are of the same kind in this case  a b and g  and 